1
00:00:00,116 --> 00:00:03,402
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:03,611 --> 00:00:07,372
In today's T-Level Chemistry lesson, we are going to study

3
00:00:07,523 --> 00:00:11,215
A eleven point three, Catalysts and Reaction Rate.

4
00:00:11,459 --> 00:00:14,663
By the end of this lesson, you should be able to define

5
00:00:14,687 --> 00:00:18,506
a catalyst accurately, use evidence to decide whether

6
00:00:18,588 --> 00:00:22,721
a substance is acting as a catalyst, explain how a catalyst

7
00:00:22,779 --> 00:00:26,912
provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy,

8
00:00:26,947 --> 00:00:30,395
and interpret that on a Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution.

9
00:00:30,569 --> 00:00:32,183
Retrieve collision theory.

10
00:00:32,461 --> 00:00:36,397
Activation energy is the minimum collision energy required

11
00:00:36,455 --> 00:00:38,080
for a successful reaction.

12
00:00:38,231 --> 00:00:41,958
Particles must collide with sufficient energy and the correct

13
00:00:42,028 --> 00:00:43,572
spatial orientation.

14
00:00:43,746 --> 00:00:47,624
At a fixed temperature, particles have a distribution of kinetic

15
00:00:47,705 --> 00:00:51,374
energies, shown by a Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution.

16
00:00:51,896 --> 00:00:56,111
How could a reaction be made faster without increasing temperature?

17
00:00:56,726 --> 00:00:59,234
One method is to use a catalyst.

18
00:00:59,478 --> 00:01:03,088
Specification point A eleven point three point one.

19
00:01:03,610 --> 00:01:07,628
A catalyst is a substance that increases the rate of a chemical

20
00:01:07,697 --> 00:01:11,621
reaction without itself being permanently chemically changed.

21
00:01:12,144 --> 00:01:16,022
The catalyst may participate in the reaction mechanism, but

22
00:01:16,091 --> 00:01:20,270
it is regenerated overall, so it is not used up permanently.

23
00:01:20,723 --> 00:01:24,473
A correct definition must include both increased rate and

24
00:01:24,589 --> 00:01:26,458
no permanent chemical change.

25
00:01:27,167 --> 00:01:30,533
A catalyst does not mean more reactant is present.

26
00:01:30,928 --> 00:01:34,342
It does not, by definition, increase the temperature.

27
00:01:34,864 --> 00:01:38,486
And it is not simply a substance that makes a reaction happen.

28
00:01:38,637 --> 00:01:42,747
The key effect is a faster reaction through a different reaction pathway.

29
00:01:42,863 --> 00:01:46,242
A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway.

30
00:01:46,416 --> 00:01:50,584
That pathway has a lower activation energy than the uncatalysed

31
00:01:50,653 --> 00:01:51,315
pathway.

32
00:01:51,489 --> 00:01:53,150
Watch the energy profile.

33
00:01:53,428 --> 00:01:56,772
The uncatalysed route has a higher energy barrier.

34
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:59,384
The catalysed route has a lower barrier.

35
00:01:59,535 --> 00:02:03,494
At the same temperature, the particles have the same overall energy

36
00:02:03,552 --> 00:02:05,236
distribution as before.

37
00:02:05,573 --> 00:02:09,648
Because the activation-energy threshold is lower, more particles

38
00:02:09,717 --> 00:02:11,796
have sufficient energy to react.

39
00:02:12,132 --> 00:02:13,874
Link that to collision theory.

40
00:02:14,083 --> 00:02:17,728
Without a catalyst, only collisions with energy at least

41
00:02:17,775 --> 00:02:21,084
the higher E a can satisfy the energy requirement.

42
00:02:21,421 --> 00:02:25,275
With a catalyst, the lower E a means a greater proportion

43
00:02:25,345 --> 00:02:27,585
of collisions have sufficient energy.

44
00:02:27,736 --> 00:02:30,592
Correct spatial orientation is still required.

45
00:02:30,871 --> 00:02:34,679
More successful collisions per unit time means an increased rate

46
00:02:34,714 --> 00:02:35,527
of reaction.

47
00:02:35,771 --> 00:02:36,839
Use evidence.

48
00:02:37,048 --> 00:02:40,682
A reaction without substance X takes one hundred and twenty

49
00:02:40,740 --> 00:02:43,155
seconds to reach the stated endpoint.

50
00:02:43,399 --> 00:02:46,429
With substance X it takes forty five seconds.

51
00:02:46,638 --> 00:02:50,539
Substance X is recovered after the reaction with no permanent

52
00:02:50,597 --> 00:02:51,642
chemical change.

53
00:02:52,036 --> 00:02:55,926
That evidence is consistent with X acting as a catalyst.

54
00:02:56,262 --> 00:02:58,608
Rate evidence alone is not enough.

55
00:02:59,002 --> 00:03:02,938
You also need evidence that the substance is not permanently chemically

56
00:03:02,996 --> 00:03:03,507
changed.

57
00:03:03,844 --> 00:03:07,524
Specification point A eleven point three point two.

58
00:03:07,861 --> 00:03:11,797
A catalyst does not need a new Maxwell-Boltzmann curve at

59
00:03:11,843 --> 00:03:12,946
the same temperature.

60
00:03:13,225 --> 00:03:17,416
The particle-energy distribution is unchanged because temperature

61
00:03:17,474 --> 00:03:18,519
is unchanged.

62
00:03:18,972 --> 00:03:21,909
What changes is the activation-energy threshold.

63
00:03:22,246 --> 00:03:25,323
The catalysed pathway has a lower E a.

64
00:03:25,659 --> 00:03:29,491
On one distribution, the catalysed E a is positioned

65
00:03:29,525 --> 00:03:32,184
to the left of the uncatalysed E a.

66
00:03:32,579 --> 00:03:36,236
The area to the right of the catalysed E a is larger,

67
00:03:36,515 --> 00:03:40,671
so a greater proportion of particles have sufficient energy to react.

68
00:03:41,379 --> 00:03:43,898
Do not confuse a catalyst with temperature.

69
00:03:44,293 --> 00:03:48,124
Increasing temperature changes the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution.

70
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:52,815
Adding a catalyst at the same temperature does not change that distribution.

71
00:03:53,431 --> 00:03:57,506
A catalyst lowers the activation energy by providing an alternative

72
00:03:57,552 --> 00:03:58,214
pathway.

73
00:03:58,736 --> 00:04:02,707
Both can increase the proportion of particles able to react, but

74
00:04:02,742 --> 00:04:04,472
they do so in different ways.

75
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:06,503
Guided application.

76
00:04:06,956 --> 00:04:10,892
A reaction has E a of eighty-five kilojoules per mole

77
00:04:11,043 --> 00:04:14,909
without a catalyst, and fifty-two kilojoules per mole with

78
00:04:14,955 --> 00:04:15,756
a catalyst.

79
00:04:16,035 --> 00:04:17,835
Temperature is unchanged.

80
00:04:18,064 --> 00:04:22,081
The fifty two kilojoules per mole line lies further left.

81
00:04:22,290 --> 00:04:24,833
The shape of the distribution does not change.

82
00:04:25,042 --> 00:04:28,734
The catalysed pathway has the larger proportion of particles

83
00:04:28,792 --> 00:04:32,820
with energy at least E a, so successful collisions occur

84
00:04:32,937 --> 00:04:35,874
more frequently and rate increases.

85
00:04:36,269 --> 00:04:38,196
Correct the misconceptions.

86
00:04:38,474 --> 00:04:41,644
A catalyst does not give particles more energy.

87
00:04:42,097 --> 00:04:46,253
At the same temperature the energy distribution is unchanged.

88
00:04:46,590 --> 00:04:50,119
A catalyst does not lower the energy of every particle.

89
00:04:50,398 --> 00:04:53,974
It lowers the activation-energy requirement.

90
00:04:54,369 --> 00:04:56,470
A catalyst is not used up.

91
00:04:56,644 --> 00:04:59,686
It is not permanently chemically changed overall.

92
00:05:00,023 --> 00:05:03,691
And a catalyst does not change rate by changing

93
00:05:03,773 --> 00:05:04,446
temperature.

94
00:05:04,783 --> 00:05:08,649
Its defining role is an alternative lower E a

95
00:05:08,765 --> 00:05:09,427
pathway.

96
00:05:09,763 --> 00:05:11,702
Examination-style question.

97
00:05:12,027 --> 00:05:12,933
Part a.

98
00:05:13,165 --> 00:05:14,697
Define a catalyst.

99
00:05:14,814 --> 00:05:15,661
Two marks.

100
00:05:15,986 --> 00:05:19,597
A substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction,

101
00:05:19,806 --> 00:05:23,172
without itself being permanently chemically changed.

102
00:05:23,567 --> 00:05:24,275
Part b.

103
00:05:24,449 --> 00:05:28,153
Explain how a catalyst increases rate in terms of

104
00:05:28,281 --> 00:05:29,802
activation energy.

105
00:05:30,011 --> 00:05:30,870
Two marks.

106
00:05:31,079 --> 00:05:34,829
It provides an alternative reaction pathway with lower

107
00:05:34,898 --> 00:05:36,187
activation energy.

108
00:05:36,338 --> 00:05:37,139
Part c.

109
00:05:37,348 --> 00:05:41,365
A Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution is drawn at one temperature.

110
00:05:41,539 --> 00:05:45,823
Explain how the graph should show catalysed and uncatalysed

111
00:05:45,916 --> 00:05:46,892
pathways.

112
00:05:47,170 --> 00:05:48,157
Three marks.

113
00:05:48,610 --> 00:05:51,779
Use the same distribution at the same temperature.

114
00:05:51,954 --> 00:05:55,274
Show a lower E a for the catalysed pathway.

115
00:05:55,889 --> 00:05:59,814
The area to the right of the catalysed E a is larger.

116
00:06:00,150 --> 00:06:01,102
Part d.

117
00:06:01,311 --> 00:06:05,282
Explain why the catalysed pathway produces a greater proportion

118
00:06:05,398 --> 00:06:07,047
of successful collisions.

119
00:06:07,499 --> 00:06:08,277
Two marks.

120
00:06:08,672 --> 00:06:12,341
A greater proportion of particles have energy at least

121
00:06:12,410 --> 00:06:16,091
the lower E a, so more collisions satisfy

122
00:06:16,184 --> 00:06:19,133
the energy requirement and can be successful.

123
00:06:19,655 --> 00:06:22,070
Before we finish, complete the model.

124
00:06:22,349 --> 00:06:26,099
A catalyst increases rate without permanent chemical change.

125
00:06:26,435 --> 00:06:30,243
It provides an alternative pathway with lower activation

126
00:06:30,313 --> 00:06:30,847
energy.

127
00:06:31,126 --> 00:06:35,015
At constant temperature the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution stays

128
00:06:35,038 --> 00:06:35,642
the same.

129
00:06:35,793 --> 00:06:39,496
The lower E a increases the area of particles

130
00:06:39,577 --> 00:06:43,734
able to react, so successful collisions occur more frequently.

